{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aoi","familyName":"Otani","name":"Otani, Aoi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Catastrophic Forgetting and Mode Collapse in Text-to-Image Diffusion via Latent Replay"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T23:45:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:01:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continual learning -- the ability to acquire knowledge incrementally without forgetting previous skills -- is fundamental to natural intelligence. While the human brain excels at this, artificial neural networks struggle with \"catastrophic forgetting,\" where learning new tasks erases previously acquired knowledge. This challenge is particularly severe for text-to-image diffusion models, which generate images from textual prompts. Additionally, these models face \"mode collapse,\" where their outputs become increasingly repetitive over time. To address these challenges, we apply Latent Replay, a neuroscience-inspired approach, to diffusion models. Traditional replay methods mitigate forgetting by storing and revisiting past examples, typically requiring large collections of images. Latent Replay instead retains only compact, high-level feature representations extracted from the model's internal architecture. This mirrors the hippocampal process of storing neural activity patterns rather than raw sensory inputs, reducing memory usage while preserving critical information. Through experiments with five sequentially learned visual concepts, we demonstrate that Latent Replay significantly outperforms existing methods in maintaining model versatility. After learning all concepts, our approach retained 77.59% Image Alignment (IA) on the earliest concept, 14% higher than baseline methods, while maintaining diverse outputs. Surprisingly, random selection of stored latent examples outperforms similarity-based strategies. Our findings suggest that Latent Replay enables efficient continual learning for generative AI models, paving the way for personalized text-to-image models that evolve with user needs without excessive computational costs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:41:24Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:41:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:41:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Vasilkovsky","name":"Vasilkovsky, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Shakhrai","name":"Shakhrai, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiye","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Peiye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Tulyakov","name":"Tulyakov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Wonka","name":"Wonka, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Ying","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hsin-Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"T2Bs: Text-to-Character Blendshapes via Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T21:04:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:11:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present T2Bs, a framework for generating high-quality, animatable character head morphable models from text by combining static text-to-3D generation with video diffusion. Text-to-3D models produce detailed static geometry but lack motion synthesis, while video diffusion models generate motion with temporal and multi-view geometric inconsistencies. T2Bs bridges this gap by leveraging deformable 3D Gaussian splatting to align static 3D assets with video outputs. By constraining motion with static geometry and employing a view-dependent deformation MLP, T2Bs (i) outperforms existing 4D generation methods in accuracy and expressiveness while reducing video artifacts and view inconsistencies, and (ii) reconstructs smooth, coherent, fully registered 3D geometries designed to scale for building morphable models with diverse, realistic facial motions. This enables synthesizing expressive, animatable character heads that surpass current 4D generation techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:52Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:10:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-Han","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chun-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendra","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable Part Diffusion 4D: Multi-View RGB and Kinematic Parts Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T23:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Stable Part Diffusion 4D (SP4D), a framework for generating paired RGB and kinematic part videos from monocular inputs. Unlike conventional part segmentation methods that rely on appearance-based semantic cues, SP4D learns to produce kinematic parts - structural components aligned with object articulation and consistent across views and time. SP4D adopts a dual-branch diffusion model that jointly synthesizes RGB frames and corresponding part segmentation maps. To simplify the architecture and flexibly enable different part counts, we introduce a spatial color encoding scheme that maps part masks to continuous RGB-like images. This encoding allows the segmentation branch to share the latent VAE from the RGB branch, while enabling part segmentation to be recovered via straightforward post-processing. A Bidirectional Diffusion Fusion (BiDiFuse) module enhances cross-branch consistency, supported by a contrastive part consistency loss to promote spatial and temporal alignment of part predictions. We demonstrate that the generated 2D part maps can be lifted to 3D to derive skeletal structures and harmonic skinning weights with few manual adjustments. To train and evaluate SP4D, we construct KinematicParts20K, a curated dataset of over 20K rigged objects selected and processed from Objaverse XL (Deitke et al., 2023), each paired with multi-view RGB and part video sequences. Experiments show that SP4D generalizes strongly to diverse scenarios, including real-world videos, novel generated objects, and rare articulated poses, producing kinematic-aware outputs suitable for downstream animation and motion-related tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Page: https://stablepartdiffusion4d.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10902","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10902","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10902","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad","familyName":"Osman","name":"Osman, Ahmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clemente","familyName":"Ibarra-Castanedo","name":"Ibarra-Castanedo, Clemente","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Mandelis","name":"Mandelis, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Maldague","name":"Maldague, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-Time Super-Resolution Imaging System Based on Zero-Shot Learning for Infrared Non-Destructive Testing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:05:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tim.2025.3647992","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infrared thermography (IRT) and photothermal coherence tomography (PCT) exhibit potential in non-destructive testing and biomedical fields. However, the inevitable heat diffusion significantly affects the sensitivity and resolution of IRT and PCT. Conventional image processing techniques rely on capturing complete thermal sequences, which limits their ability to achieve real-time processes. Here, we construct a real-time super-resolution imaging system based on zero-shot learning strategy for the non-invasive infrared thermography and photothermal coherence tomography techniques. To validate the feasibility and accuracy of this super-resolution imaging system, IRT systems were employed to test several industrial samples and one biomedical sample. The results demonstrated high contrast in the region of interest (ROI) and uncovered valuable information otherwise obscured by thermal diffusion. Furthermore, three-dimensional photothermal coherence tomography was used to validate the excellent denoising and deconvolution capabilities of the proposed real-time super-resolution imaging system."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10902","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:08Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:17:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToMA: Token Merge with Attention for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-30T17:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel in high-fidelity image generation but face scalability limits due to transformers' quadratic attention complexity. Plug-and-play token reduction methods like ToMeSD and ToFu reduce FLOPs by merging redundant tokens in generated images but rely on GPU-inefficient operations (e.g., sorting, scattered writes), introducing overheads that negate theoretical speedups when paired with optimized attention implementations (e.g., FlashAttention). To bridge this gap, we propose Token Merge with Attention (ToMA), an off-the-shelf method that redesigns token reduction for GPU-aligned efficiency, with three key contributions: 1) a reformulation of token merge as a submodular optimization problem to select diverse tokens; 2) merge/unmerge as an attention-like linear transformation via GPU-friendly matrix operations; and 3) exploiting latent locality and sequential redundancy (pattern reuse) to minimize overhead. ToMA reduces SDXL/Flux generation latency by 24%/23%, respectively (with DINO $Δ\u0026lt; 0.07$), outperforming prior methods. This work bridges the gap between theoretical and practical efficiency for transformers in diffusion. Code available at https://github.com/WenboLuu/ToMA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025). Code available at https://github.com/wenboluu/ToMA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Xiang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hao-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PanoLora: Bridging Perspective and Panoramic Video Generation with LoRA Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:05:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating high-quality 360° panoramic videos remains a significant challenge due to the fundamental differences between panoramic and traditional perspective-view projections. While perspective videos rely on a single viewpoint with a limited field of view, panoramic content requires rendering the full surrounding environment, making it difficult for standard video generation models to adapt. Existing solutions often introduce complex architectures or large-scale training, leading to inefficiency and suboptimal results. Motivated by the success of Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) in style transfer tasks, we propose treating panoramic video generation as an adaptation problem from perspective views. Through theoretical analysis, we demonstrate that LoRA can effectively model the transformation between these projections when its rank exceeds the degrees of freedom in the task. Our approach efficiently fine-tunes a pretrained video diffusion model using only approximately 1,000 videos while achieving high-quality panoramic generation. Experimental results demonstrate that our method maintains proper projection geometry and surpasses previous state-of-the-art approaches in visual quality, left-right consistency, and motion diversity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Daiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Pingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Sliders: Mastering the Art of Diffusion-based Image Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T10:48:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:41:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the realm of image generation, the quest for realism and customization has never been more pressing. While existing methods like concept sliders have made strides, they often falter when it comes to no-AIGC images, particularly images captured in real world settings. To bridge this gap, we introduce Beyond Sliders, an innovative framework that integrates GANs and diffusion models to facilitate sophisticated image manipulation across diverse image categories. Improved upon concept sliders, our method refines the image through fine grained guidance both textual and visual in an adversarial manner, leading to a marked enhancement in image quality and realism. Extensive experimental validation confirms the robustness and versatility of Beyond Sliders across a spectrum of applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:57:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Ajoudani","name":"Ajoudani, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ActivePose: Active 6D Object Pose Estimation and Tracking for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:50:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T13:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:44:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 6-DoF object pose estimation and tracking are critical for reliable robotic manipulation. However, zero-shot methods often fail under viewpoint-induced ambiguities and fixed-camera setups struggle when objects move or become self-occluded. To address these challenges, we propose an active pose estimation pipeline that combines a Vision-Language Model (VLM) with \"robotic imagination\" to dynamically detect and resolve ambiguities in real time. In an offline stage, we render a dense set of views of the CAD model, compute the FoundationPose entropy for each view, and construct a geometric-aware prompt that includes low-entropy (unambiguous) and high-entropy (ambiguous) examples. At runtime, the system: (1) queries the VLM on the live image for an ambiguity score; (2) if ambiguity is detected, imagines a discrete set of candidate camera poses by rendering virtual views, scores each based on a weighted combination of VLM ambiguity probability and FoundationPose entropy, and then moves the camera to the Next-Best-View (NBV) to obtain a disambiguated pose estimation. Furthermore, since moving objects may leave the camera's field of view, we introduce an active pose tracking module: a diffusion-policy trained via imitation learning, which generates camera trajectories that preserve object visibility and minimize pose ambiguity. Experiments in simulation and real-world show that our approach significantly outperforms classical baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6D Pose, Diffusion Policy, Robot Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:58Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11487","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11487","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11487","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rashid","familyName":"Mushkani","name":"Mushkani, Rashid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collective Recourse for Generative Urban Visualizations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:39:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:04:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-01T03:00:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:22:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image diffusion models help visualize urban futures but can amplify group-level harms. We propose collective recourse: structured community \"visual bug reports\" that trigger fixes to models and planning workflows. We (1) formalize collective recourse and a practical pipeline (report, triage, fix, verify, closure); (2) situate four recourse primitives within the diffusion stack: counter-prompts, negative prompts, dataset edits, and reward-model tweaks; (3) define mandate thresholds via a mandate score combining severity, volume saturation, representativeness, and evidence; and (4) evaluate a synthetic program of 240 reports. Prompt-level fixes were fastest (median 2.1-3.4 days) but less durable (21-38% recurrence); dataset edits and reward tweaks were slower (13.5 and 21.9 days) yet more durable (12-18% recurrence) with higher planner uptake (30-36%). A threshold of 0.12 yielded 93% precision and 75% recall; increasing representativeness raised recall to 81% with little precision loss. We discuss integration with participatory governance, risks (e.g., overfitting to vocal groups), and safeguards (dashboards, rotating juries)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11487","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:50Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milan","familyName":"Marocchi","name":"Marocchi, Milan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Fynn","name":"Fynn, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kayapanda","familyName":"Mandana","name":"Mandana, Kayapanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Rong","name":"Rong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling to Multimodal and Multichannel Heart Sound Classification with Synthetic and Augmented Biosignals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T05:52:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T03:27:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:27:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T09:10:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:21:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-02T08:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are the leading cause of death worldwide, accounting for approximately 17.9 million deaths each year. Early detection is critical, creating a demand for accurate and inexpensive pre-screening methods. Deep learning has recently been applied to classify abnormal heart sounds indicative of CVDs using synchronised phonocardiogram (PCG) and electrocardiogram (ECG) signals, as well as multichannel PCG (mPCG). However, state-of-the-art architectures remain underutilised due to the limited availability of synchronised and multichannel datasets. Augmented datasets and pre-trained models provide a pathway to overcome these limitations, enabling transformer-based architectures to be trained effectively. This work combines traditional signal processing with denoising diffusion models, WaveGrad and DiffWave, to create an augmented dataset to fine-tune a Wav2Vec 2.0-based classifier on multimodal and multichannel heart sound datasets. The approach achieves state-of-the-art performance. On the Computing in Cardiology (CinC) 2016 dataset of single channel PCG, accuracy, unweighted average recall (UAR), sensitivity, specificity and Matthew's correlation coefficient (MCC) reach 92.48%, 93.05%, 93.63%, 92.48%, 94.93% and 0.8283, respectively. Using the synchronised PCG and ECG signals of the training-a dataset from CinC, 93.14%, 92.21%, 94.35%, 90.10%, 95.12% and 0.8380 are achieved for accuracy, UAR, sensitivity, specificity and MCC, respectively. Using a wearable vest dataset consisting of mPCG data, the model achieves 77.13% accuracy, 74.25% UAR, 86.47% sensitivity, 62.04% specificity, and 0.5082 MCC. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of transformer-based models for CVD detection when supported by augmented datasets, highlighting their potential to advance multimodal and multichannel heart sound classification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages, 37 figures, 19 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:06:37Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:06:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:12:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhuiyi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yuanhuiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaobo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpeCa: Accelerating Diffusion Transformers with Speculative Feature Caching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T06:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:05:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746027.3755331","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have revolutionized high-fidelity image and video synthesis, yet their computational demands remain prohibitive for real-time applications. These models face two fundamental challenges: strict temporal dependencies preventing parallelization, and computationally intensive forward passes required at each denoising step. Drawing inspiration from speculative decoding in large language models, we present SpeCa, a novel 'Forecast-then-verify' acceleration framework that effectively addresses both limitations. SpeCa's core innovation lies in introducing Speculative Sampling to diffusion models, predicting intermediate features for subsequent timesteps based on fully computed reference timesteps. Our approach implements a parameter-free verification mechanism that efficiently evaluates prediction reliability, enabling real-time decisions to accept or reject each prediction while incurring negligible computational overhead. Furthermore, SpeCa introduces sample-adaptive computation allocation that dynamically modulates resources based on generation complexity, allocating reduced computation for simpler samples while preserving intensive processing for complex instances. Experiments demonstrate 6.34x acceleration on FLUX with minimal quality degradation (5.5% drop), 7.3x speedup on DiT while preserving generation fidelity, and 79.84% VBench score at 6.1x acceleration for HunyuanVideo. The verification mechanism incurs minimal overhead (1.67%-3.5% of full inference costs), establishing a new paradigm for efficient diffusion model inference while maintaining generation quality even at aggressive acceleration ratios. Our codes have been released in Github: \\textbf{https://github.com/Shenyi-Z/Cache4Diffusion}"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures, ACM Multimedia 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:07:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11661","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11661","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11661","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifei","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Lifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Baoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuandong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DTGen: Generative Diffusion-Based Few-Shot Data Augmentation for Fine-Grained Dirty Tableware Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T07:59:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:08:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Intelligent tableware cleaning is a critical application in food safety and smart homes, but existing methods are limited by coarse-grained classification and scarcity of few-shot data, making it difficult to meet industrialization requirements. We propose DTGen, a few-shot data augmentation scheme based on generative diffusion models, specifically designed for fine-grained dirty tableware recognition. DTGen achieves efficient domain specialization through LoRA, generates diverse dirty images via structured prompts, and ensures data quality through CLIP-based cross-modal filtering. Under extremely limited real few-shot conditions, DTGen can synthesize virtually unlimited high-quality samples, significantly improving classifier performance and supporting fine-grained dirty tableware recognition. We further elaborate on lightweight deployment strategies, promising to transfer DTGen's benefits to embedded dishwashers and integrate with cleaning programs to intelligently regulate energy consumption and detergent usage. Research results demonstrate that DTGen not only validates the value of generative AI in few-shot industrial vision but also provides a feasible deployment path for automated tableware cleaning and food safety monitoring."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11661","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:08:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:08:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:08:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wa-Kin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Wa-Kin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun-Cheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jun-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang-Tse","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shang-Tse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DRAG: Data Reconstruction Attack using Guided Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T09:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:12:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rise of large foundation models, split inference (SI) has emerged as a popular computational paradigm for deploying models across lightweight edge devices and cloud servers, addressing data privacy and computational cost concerns. However, most existing data reconstruction attacks have focused on smaller CNN classification models, leaving the privacy risks of foundation models in SI settings largely unexplored. To address this gap, we propose a novel data reconstruction attack based on guided diffusion, which leverages the rich prior knowledge embedded in a latent diffusion model (LDM) pre-trained on a large-scale dataset. Our method performs iterative reconstruction on the LDM's learned image prior, effectively generating high-fidelity images resembling the original data from their intermediate representations (IR). Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods, both qualitatively and quantitatively, in reconstructing data from deep-layer IRs of the vision foundation model. The results highlight the urgent need for more robust privacy protection mechanisms for large models in SI scenarios. Code is available at: https://github.com/ntuaislab/DRAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:09:35Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:09:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:09:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiduo","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haiduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuwei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanwu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xuanwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengju","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecVLM: Fast Speculative Decoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:18:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-21T03:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:45:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speculative decoding is a powerful way to accelerate autoregressive large language models (LLMs), but directly porting it to vision-language models (VLMs) faces unique systems constraints: the prefill stage is dominated by visual tokens whose count scales with image resolution and video length, inflating both compute and memory, especially the key-value (KV) cache. We study speculative decoding for VLMs and introduce SpecVLM, a practical system that (1) establishes a strong EAGLE-2-style baseline, EagleVLM, delivering 1.5--2.3x end-to-end speedups over full autoregressive inference, and (2) further accelerates VLM inference with an elastic visual compressor that adaptively selects among pruning, pooling, convolution, and resampler primitives to balance FLOPs/parameters and accuracy per input. To avoid costly offline distillation corpora, we propose an online-logit distillation protocol that trains the draft model with on-the-fly teacher logits and penultimate features using a combined cross-entropy and Smooth L1 objective, eliminating storage and preprocessing while remaining compute-efficient. This protocol reveals a training-time scaling effect: longer online training monotonically increases the draft model's average accepted length, improving speculative efficiency. Empirically, SpecVLM achieves additional acceleration, culminating in 2.5--2.9x end-to-end speedups within 5 epochs across LLaVA and MMMU, consistently over resolutions and task difficulties, while preserving the target model's output distribution (lossless decoding). Our code is available at https://github.com/haiduo/SpecVLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:11:51Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:11:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Travis","familyName":"Davies","name":"Davies, Travis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yunxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huxian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luhui","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Luhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tenma: Robust Cross-Embodiment Robot Manipulation with Diffusion Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:39:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:20:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling Transformer policies and diffusion models has advanced robotic manipulation, yet combining these techniques in lightweight, cross-embodiment learning settings remains challenging. We study design choices that most affect stability and performance for diffusion-transformer policies trained on heterogeneous, multimodal robot data, and introduce Tenma, a lightweight diffusion-transformer for bi-manual arm control. Tenma integrates multiview RGB, proprioception, and language via a cross-embodiment normalizer that maps disparate state/action spaces into a shared latent space; a Joint State-Time encoder for temporally aligned observation learning with inference speed boosts; and a diffusion action decoder optimized for training stability and learning capacity. Across benchmarks and under matched compute, Tenma achieves an average success rate of 88.95% in-distribution and maintains strong performance under object and scene shifts, substantially exceeding baseline policies whose best in-distribution average is 18.12%. Despite using moderate data scale, Tenma delivers robust manipulation and generalization, indicating the great potential for multimodal and cross-embodiment learning strategies for further augmenting the capacity of transformer-based imitation learning policies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:03Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:13:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Jamil","name":"Jamil, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kotla Sai","familyName":"Charan","name":"Charan, Kotla Sai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriparna","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sriparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koustava","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Koustava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K J","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, K J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do It Yourself (DIY): Modifying Images for Poems in a Zero-Shot Setting Using Weighted Prompt Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T12:58:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:21:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Poetry is an expressive form of art that invites multiple interpretations, as readers often bring their own emotions, experiences, and cultural backgrounds into their understanding of a poem. Recognizing this, we aim to generate images for poems and improve these images in a zero-shot setting, enabling audiences to modify images as per their requirements. To achieve this, we introduce a novel Weighted Prompt Manipulation (WPM) technique, which systematically modifies attention weights and text embeddings within diffusion models. By dynamically adjusting the importance of specific words, WPM enhances or suppresses their influence in the final generated image, leading to semantically richer and more contextually accurate visualizations. Our approach exploits diffusion models and large language models (LLMs) such as GPT in conjunction with existing poetry datasets, ensuring a comprehensive and structured methodology for improved image generation in the literary domain. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt at integrating weighted prompt manipulation for enhancing imagery in poetic language."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:13:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:13:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:13:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Gaa","name":"Gaa, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Weickert","name":"Weickert, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iva","familyName":"Farag","name":"Farag, Iva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Özgün","familyName":"Çiçek","name":"Çiçek, Özgün","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Filter Echo: A General Tool for Filter Visualisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T13:47:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:24:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-03T06:41:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:41:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10851-026-01298-6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To select suitable filters for a task or to improve existing filters, a deep understanding of their inner workings is vital. Diffusion echoes, which are space-adaptive impulse responses, are useful to visualise the effect of nonlinear diffusion filters. However, they have received little attention in the literature. There may be two reasons for this: Firstly, the concept was introduced specifically for diffusion filters, which might appear too limited. Secondly, diffusion echoes have large storage requirements, which restricts their practicality. This work addresses both problems. We introduce the filter echo as a generalisation of the diffusion echo and use it for applications beyond adaptive smoothing, such as image inpainting, osmosis, and variational optic flow computation. We provide a framework to visualise and inspect echoes from various filters with different applications. Furthermore, we propose a compression approach for filter echoes, which reduces storage requirements by a factor of 20 to 100."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:14:44Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:14:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:14:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Youquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyue","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaici","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huaici","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Tsang","familyName":"Ooi","name":"Ooi, Wei Tsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Generate 4D LiDAR Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While generative world models have advanced video and occupancy-based data synthesis, LiDAR generation remains underexplored despite its importance for accurate 3D perception. Extending generation to 4D LiDAR data introduces challenges in controllability, temporal stability, and evaluation. We present LiDARCrafter, a unified framework that converts free-form language into editable LiDAR sequences. Instructions are parsed into ego-centric scene graphs, which a tri-branch diffusion model transforms into object layouts, trajectories, and shapes. A range-image diffusion model generates the initial scan, and an autoregressive module extends it into a temporally coherent sequence. The explicit layout design further supports object-level editing, such as insertion or relocation. To enable fair assessment, we provide EvalSuite, a benchmark spanning scene-, object-, and sequence-level metrics. On nuScenes, LiDARCrafter achieves state-of-the-art fidelity, controllability, and temporal consistency, offering a foundation for LiDAR-based simulation and data augmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Abstract Paper (Non-Archival) @ ICCV 2025 Wild3D Workshop; GitHub Repo at https://lidarcrafter.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huilin","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Huilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec-LLaVA: Accelerating Vision-Language Models with Dynamic Tree-Based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable powerful multimodal reasoning but suffer from slow autoregressive inference, limiting their deployment in real-time applications. We introduce Spec-LLaVA, a system that applies speculative decoding to accelerate VLMs without sacrificing output quality. Spec-LLaVA pairs a lightweight draft VLM with a large target model: the draft speculates future tokens, which the target verifies in parallel, allowing multiple tokens to be generated per step. To maximize efficiency, we design a dynamic tree-based verification algorithm that adaptively expands and prunes speculative branches using draft model confidence. On MS COCO out-of-domain images, Spec-LLaVA achieves up to 3.28$\\times$ faster decoding on LLaVA-1.5 (7B, 13B) with no loss in generation quality. This work presents a lossless acceleration framework for VLMs using dynamic tree-structured speculative decoding, opening a path toward practical real-time multimodal assistants. Importantly, the lightweight draft model design makes the framework amenable to resource-constrained or on-device deployment settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7pages, accepted by ICML TTODLer-FM workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Finn","familyName":"Carter","name":"Carter, Finn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyue","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Chenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Ku","name":"Ku, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Daheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models: A Theoretical Perspective on Security and Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:05:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T12:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved unprecedented success in image generation but pose increasing risks in terms of privacy, fairness, and security. A growing demand exists to \\emph{erase} sensitive or harmful concepts (e.g., NSFW content, private individuals, artistic styles) from these models while preserving their overall generative capabilities. We introduce \\textbf{SCORE} (Secure and Concept-Oriented Robust Erasure), a novel framework for robust concept removal in diffusion models. SCORE formulates concept erasure as an \\emph{adversarial independence} problem, theoretically guaranteeing that the model's outputs become statistically independent of the erased concept. Unlike prior heuristic methods, SCORE minimizes the mutual information between a target concept and generated outputs, yielding provable erasure guarantees. We provide formal proofs establishing convergence properties and derive upper bounds on residual concept leakage. Empirically, we evaluate SCORE on Stable Diffusion and FLUX across four challenging benchmarks: object erasure, NSFW removal, celebrity face suppression, and artistic style unlearning. SCORE consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods including EraseAnything, ANT, MACE, ESD, and UCE, achieving up to \\textbf{12.5\\%} higher erasure efficacy while maintaining comparable or superior image quality. By integrating adversarial optimization, trajectory consistency, and saliency-driven fine-tuning, SCORE sets a new standard for secure and robust concept erasure in diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"updated version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:25:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Isobe","name":"Isobe, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baolu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baolu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qinghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiwen","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Haiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huchuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emad","familyName":"Barsoum","name":"Barsoum, Emad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Layout-Conditioned Autoregressive Text-to-Image Generation via Structured Masking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:27:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-30T12:19:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:54:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-04T12:10:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although autoregressive (AR) models have demonstrated remarkable success in image generation, extending these models to layout-conditioned generation remains challenging due to the sparse nature of layout conditions and the risk of feature entanglement. We present \\textbf{S}tructured \\textbf{M}asking for \\textbf{AR}-based \\textbf{L}ayout-to-\\textbf{I}mage (SMARLI), a novel framework that effectively integrates spatial layout constraints into the AR generation process. To equip AR models with layout control, a structured masking strategy is applied to the attention computation to govern the interaction among the global prompt, layout, and image tokens. This design prevents the misassociation of different regions with their corresponding descriptions while enabling the sufficient injection of layout constraints into the generation process. To alleviate the exposure bias of AR models and further enhance generation quality and layout accuracy, we incorporate a Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) post-training scheme. We adapt it to the next-set-based paradigm and introduce a specifically designed layout reward, which is coordinated with an image quality reward to guide policy optimization in a balanced manner. Experimental results demonstrate that SMARLI seamlessly integrates layout tokens with text and image tokens without compromising generation quality, and the proposed masking strategy and post-training scheme can also be transferred to standard next-token-based AR models. The proposed framework achieves superior layout control while maintaining the structural simplicity and generation efficiency of AR models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ECCV 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:57:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Tao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hai-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Suiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxing","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FluentAvatar: Flicker-Free Talking-Head Animation via Phoneme-Guided Autoregressive Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:34:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T16:37:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:05:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T18:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current talking-head generation has gradually shifted from GAN-based methods to diffusion-based paradigms, achieving remarkable progress in visual fidelity and temporal consistency. However, inter-frame flicker remains prevalent in existing diffusion-based methods. An important reason is that denoising trajectory variation induced by stochastic initialization leaves residual inter-frame inconsistencies, which manifest as short-term, abrupt visual fluctuations between adjacent frames. To further verify this, we conduct a controlled study by fixing the input while varying only the random seed. The results show markedly different flicker patterns across samplings, with a mean inter-seed Pearson correlation of only r = 0.15. This motivates us to explore autoregressive generation, which models frames sequentially and provides a more direct prior for temporal continuity. Based on this, we propose FluentAvatar, a two-stage autoregressive framework built on phoneme representations. First, Facial Keyframe Generation produces phoneme-aligned keyframes under a Phoneme-Frame Causal Attention Mask, and Inter-frame Interpolation synthesizes transition frames via a timestamp-aware adaptive strategy built upon selective state space modeling. Moreover, we introduce BG-Flicker, a background-isolated metric for talking-head videos that enables more reliable evaluation of inter-frame flicker. Experiments on CMLR and HDTF demonstrate that FluentAvatar achieves strong performance in visual fidelity, lip synchronization, and temporal stability, attaining the best FVD on both datasets and BG-Flicker results close to ground truth. The code, the model, and the interface will be released to facilitate further research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjie","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Mengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Progressive Flow-inspired Unfolding for Spectral Compressive Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:10:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:34:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coded aperture snapshot spectral imaging (CASSI) retrieves a 3D hyperspectral image (HSI) from a single 2D compressed measurement, which is a highly challenging reconstruction task. Recent deep unfolding networks (DUNs), empowered by explicit data-fidelity updates and implicit deep denoisers, have achieved the state of the art in CASSI reconstruction. However, existing unfolding approaches suffer from uncontrollable reconstruction trajectories, leading to abrupt quality jumps and non-gradual refinement across stages. Inspired by diffusion trajectories and flow matching, we propose a novel trajectory-controllable unfolding framework that enforces smooth, continuous optimization paths from noisy initial estimates to high-quality reconstructions. To achieve computational efficiency, we design an efficient spatial-spectral Transformer tailored for hyperspectral reconstruction, along with a frequency-domain fusion module to gurantee feature consistency. Experiments on simulation and real data demonstrate that our method achieves better reconstruction quality and efficiency than prior state-of-the-art approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:18:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12203","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12203","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12203","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xili","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duomin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Duomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xianfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel M.","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Lionel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heung-Yeung","familyName":"Shum","name":"Shum, Heung-Yeung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LazyDrag: Enabling Stable Drag-Based Editing on Multi-Modal Diffusion Transformers via Explicit Correspondence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:42:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T03:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:20:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T11:16:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reliance on implicit point matching via attention has become a core bottleneck in drag-based editing, resulting in a fundamental compromise on weakened inversion strength and costly test-time optimization (TTO). This compromise severely limits the generative capabilities of diffusion models, suppressing high-fidelity inpainting and text-guided creation. In this paper, we introduce LazyDrag, the first drag-based image editing method for Multi-Modal Diffusion Transformers, which directly eliminates the reliance on implicit point matching. In concrete terms, our method generates an explicit correspondence map from user drag inputs as a reliable reference to boost the attention control. This reliable reference opens the potential for a stable full-strength inversion process, which is the first in the drag-based editing task. It obviates the necessity for TTO and unlocks the generative capability of models. Therefore, LazyDrag naturally unifies precise geometric control with text guidance, enabling complex edits that were previously out of reach: opening the mouth of a dog and inpainting its interior, generating new objects like a ``tennis ball'', or for ambiguous drags, making context-aware changes like moving a hand into a pocket. Additionally, LazyDrag supports multi-round workflows with simultaneous move and scale operations. Evaluated on the DragBench, our method outperforms baselines in drag accuracy and perceptual quality, as validated by VIEScore and human evaluation. LazyDrag not only establishes new state-of-the-art performance, but also paves a new way to editing paradigms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://zxyin.github.io/LazyDrag"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12203","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:40:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":24,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28diffusion+OR+%22flow+matching%22+OR+autoregressive%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28diffusion+OR+%22flow+matching%22+OR+autoregressive%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28image+OR+images+OR+video+OR+videos+OR+multimodal%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28image+OR+images+OR+video+OR+videos+OR+multimodal%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000\u0026sort=name"}}